      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (60%):</strong> The dominant trajectory: FN2 (cessation by Nov 30) at 25% inverse is 75% no cessation; F3 (indirect session by Sep 17) at 7% forecloses near-term diplomacy; F6 (additional strikes by Sep 9) at 60% captures the active kinetic exchange cycle driving further escalation risk.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (30%):</strong> A sustained low-level bilateral exchange — neither side crossing a decisive threshold (nuclear facility strikes, GCC territorial seizure, US personnel killed in large numbers) — with Hormuz staying effectively closed (FN1 at 20%) and the post-October-27 Israeli election producing a shift that creates diplomatic space.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (10%):</strong> Dependent almost entirely on FN2 (cessation by Nov 30, p=0.25) resolving YES; requires both an Iranian halt to maritime attacks and a US-side diplomatic signal, neither of which has any observable precursor in the September 4-6 evidence window.</p>
